<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Richard Wagner</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1813">1813</a>–<a href="/years/1883">1883</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Richard Wagner was a German composer and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> director whose operas, which he called music dramas, transformed European music. His works include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tristan-und-isolde">Tristan und Isolde</a>, Die Meistersinger von Nürnberg, the four-part Ring cycle and Parsifal. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludwig-ii-of-bavaria">Ludwig II of Bavaria</a> became his patron in 1864, paid his debts and helped finance the festival theatre at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayreuth">Bayreuth</a>, opened in 1876. His antisemitic writings have overshadowed his legacy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/richard-wagner.jpg" alt="Richard Wagner" width="320" height="443" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Franz Hanfstaengl · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:RichardWagner.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Wilhelm Richard Wagner was born on 22 May 1813 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a> into a family of musicians, the ninth and youngest child of Carl Friedrich Wagner, a clerk in the Leipzig police service, and Johanna Rosine Wagner, a baker's daughter. His father died of typhoid fever six months after his birth. His mother then lived with Carl's friend, the actor and playwright Ludwig Geyer, whom she probably married in August 1814, and the family moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a>. Until he was fourteen Wagner was known as Wilhelm Richard Geyer, and he almost certainly believed Geyer to be his biological father.</p><p>Geyer's love of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> was shared by his stepson. At nine Wagner was greatly impressed by Carl Maria von Weber's opera Der Freischütz, which he saw Weber conduct. His first creative effort was a tragedy called Leubald, begun in 1826, and he persuaded his family to let him take music lessons. Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a> he studied harmony from 1828 to 1831. Hearing Beethoven's 7th and 9th symphonies in 1828 inspired him, and he wrote a piano transcription of the 9th.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Wagner wrote both the libretti, which he called poems, and the music of all his stage works. His earliest attempts at opera were often left unfinished. Rienzi (1842) was his first opera to be staged successfully, and he later said he did not consider his early works part of his oeuvre.</p><p>From 1849 he urged a new concept of opera, often called music drama, in which musical, poetic and dramatic elements were fused in a Gesamtkunstwerk, or total work of art. He set out these ideas in essays published between 1849 and 1852 and realised them most fully in the first half of The Ring of the Nibelung. The orchestra became as important as the singers, and leitmotifs, musical phrases tied to characters, places and plot elements, are interwoven through the drama. To stage his works he had the Bayreuth Festspielhaus built, which hosted the premieres of The Ring and Parsifal.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Wagner died on 13 February 1883. After his death his wife Cosima took over the leadership of the Bayreuth Festival, which has since remained under the management of their descendants. From <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tristan-und-isolde">Tristan und Isolde</a> onwards he explored the limits of the traditional tonal system, and some music historians date the beginning of modern classical music to its first notes. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anton-bruckner">Anton Bruckner</a>, Hugo Wolf, Richard Strauss and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-mahler">Gustav Mahler</a> were among the composers indebted to him.</p><p>He remained controversial in his lifetime and afterwards. His hostile writings on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, including Jewishness in Music, correspond to trends of thought in 19th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, though he also had Jewish friends and supporters. The extent to which antisemitic stereotypes appear in his operas is debated.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Richard Wagner” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ludwig II made Munich the scene of <b>Richard Wagner</b>'s Tristan und Isolde in 1865 and helped finance the festival theatre at Bayreuth, and his castles of Neuschwanstein, Linderhof and Herrenchiemsee became some of the most visited sights in Europe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bavaria">Kingdom of Bavaria · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Richard Wagner</b> drew on the Eddas and related sagas for his opera cycle The Ring of the Nibelung, and J.R.R. Tolkien, a scholar of Old Norse, took the names of his dwarves and of Gandalf from the Völuspá.</blockquote><a href="/religion/norse-religion">Norse religion · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Nineteenth-century Romantics and nationalists made them heroic ancestors, and the horned helmets of popular imagination come from that age, notably from the costumes for <b>Richard Wagner</b>’s Ring cycle at Bayreuth in 1876; the only nearly complete Viking helmet, found at Gjermundbu in Norway in 1943, has none.</blockquote><a href="/culture/vikings">Vikings · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>In music the Romantic style lived on through <b>Richard Wagner</b>, Johannes Brahms and Pyotr Ilyich Tchaikovsky to the end of the century.</blockquote><a href="/movement/romanticism">Romanticism · Outcomes</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Richard Wagner</b> later built his festival theatre in the town.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-brandenburg-bayreuth">Principality of Brandenburg-Bayreuth</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.34,12.38&amp;year=1858" aria-label="Leipzig on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Richard Wagner"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5656 5676h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5656 5676h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.34,12.38&amp;year=1858">What was here in 1858? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Beethoven’s Ninth Symphony became the mystical goal of all my strange thoughts and desires about music.&#8221;</p><footer>My Life (written 1865-1880), part 1 &#183; authorised English translation, 1911 (translator not named) &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/5197/pg5197-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;As my musical instruction also did me no good, I continued in my wilful process of self-education by copying out the scores of my beloved masters, and in so doing acquired a neat handwriting, which in later years has often been admired.&#8221;</p><footer>My Life (written 1865-1880), part 1 &#183; authorised English translation, 1911 (translator not named) &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/5197/pg5197-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
